  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Humanitarian Assistance to Vulnerable Migrants in Yemen</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the escalation of conflict in March 2015, Yemen continues to face the world&apos;s largest humanitarian crisis. In 2021, more than 20 million people are in need (two-thirds of the population), over 12 million of whom are in acute need of humanitarian assistance (Yemen Humanitarian Needs Overview 2021). Migrants are amongst the most marginalized, excluded, and vulnerable groups of people in need in Yemen and face considerable xenophobic and discriminatory treatment. The ongoing crisis and conflict, combined with the hardening attitudes of authorities against migrants, has increased migrants? exposure to abuse and violence. Through this project, IOM aims to contribute to address the urgent needs of migrants on the move or stranded in Yemen. IOM is targeting vulnerable migrants with urgent protection support (including emergency assistance) and healthcare services through static Migrant Response Points and mobile teams. IOM is also contributing to informing the overall humanitarian and protection response by employing its Flow Monitoring activities, through its Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) programme.</narrative>
  </description>
